referenceSolutions.python:
def rosterProofFirstQualifyingSlip(readings, k):
    a=readings
    out=[]
    n=len(a)
    def count(row,used):
        if row==n: return 1
        ways=0
        for c in range(n):
            if (a[row] & (1<<c)) and not (used & (1<<c)): ways+=count(row+1,used | (1<<c))
        return ways
    for c in range(n): out.append(count(1,1<<c) if a[0] & (1<<c) else 0)
    return next((x for x in out if x>=k),-1)

referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int rosterProofFirstQualifyingSlip(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int n=a.length;class Walk {int count(int row,int used){if(row==n)return 1;int ways=0;for(int c=0;c<n;c++)if((a[row]&(1<<c))!=0&&(used&(1<<c))==0)ways+=count(row+1,used|(1<<c));return ways;}}Walk w=new Walk();for(int c=0;c<n;c++)out.add((a[0]&(1<<c))!=0?w.count(1,1<<c):0);}
    for(int x:out)if(x>=k)return x;return -1;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
The working idea

There are n desks and n clerks. readings[row] is an n-bit permission mask for desk row. Assign one distinct clerk to each desk, using only allowed bits. For each possible clerk at desk 0, produce the number of complete assignments with that first clerk; forbidden first choices contribute 0. An empty roster produces no counts.

Return the first value in the derived sequence that is at least k, or -1 if there is none. Return the value, not its index.

Why this works

Each derived value follows the unit's stated order and includes exactly the items allowed by its definition. The receipt then folds or filters those values without changing their meaning. Check the empty, singleton, repeated and maximum-size inputs before trusting the final total.

Target time O(n*n!); space O(n).

